ed-prefix checkpoint workflow, direct and interpolated both.\" The \"DEFAULT rotating/lineartrace coupled case\" — hmm, \"rotating\" = corotational rotating MPC map, \"linear trace\" = linear displacement trace? I think this refers to the plastic DC case. The checkpoint workflow targets the plastic case. I'll restrict --checkpoint-out and --restart to --case plastic, rejecting with preyield clearly. That's a clean, defensible boundary: \"checkpoint workflow is delivered for the registered plastic displacement-controlled case\".\n\nHmm wait, but also `--stage prefix` without `--checkpoint-out`? A prefix run without saving a checkpoint is pointless but not incompatible — it's a valid partial solve. Allow it (it reports completed=false? No wait — prefix reaches its target -0.10, so status=completed with target -0.10). The JSON \"completed\" for prefix should mean: reached the prefix target. The payload should include requested/achieved displacement and control error for plastic.\n\n\"JSON add requested/achieved displacement/control error for plastic; completed requires actual status completed, target error <=1e-8 and existing constraints pass.\"\n\nSo for plastic: requested_displacement (target), achieved_displacement (probe uz at end), control_error = |achieved - requested| (or the solver's control error?). \"targeterror<=1e-8\" — |achieved - requested| <= 1e-8. And constraint postcheck passes.\n\nFor the resumed full run: requested = -11/60, achieved = probe uz.\n\nNow the restart flow: `--restart PATH` loads checkpoint bytes from file, passes to solve_static_nonlinear(restart_checkpoint=...). What does load_nonlinear_checkpoint accept — bytes? Let me check its signature. Also need to check what the resumed call needs: same model (rebuilt fresh), load case, control=\"displacement\", displacement_control with new target -11/60, num_steps=5, max_iterations=40, tolerance=1e-8. The checkpoint validation requires the analysis contract to match: kinematics corotational, num_layers, convergence settings, control row (full_row must match), load case descriptor must match. So the example rebuilds the same model and passes matching parameters.\n\nAlso: does the resumed run need `emit_restart_checkpoint=True`? If --checkpoint-out given with --restart, we'd emit again. Fine.\n\nNow, what does load_nonlinear_checkpoint accept as input? Let me check lines 1004-1063 of nonlinear_restart.py.\n\nAlso the \"input purity\" checks: \"Cancellation observes public event['type'] or typed.event_type (NOT mapping event_type); accepted metadata and input checkpoint preserved.\" — For the resumed run with cancellation, the input checkpoint bytes must be preserved (not mutated), and after cancellation a fresh resume from the same checkpoint must still work.\n\n\"Core force control cancellation DOES poll each Newton iteration (source 7453)\" — that's about the preyield force control; the existing test already covers actual cancellation. For slice2, cancellation tests: \"Add actual rejected-increment/cancellation input purity checks and failed-terminal restart refusal\".\n\nTest plan (tests/test_beam_shell_checkpoint_workflow.py):\n- CLI in separate processes/fresh models for each direct/interpolated full, prefix, resume.\n- Compare full vs resumed: displacement, load factor, all material history using EXISTING continuation tolerances (u/lambda rtol 1e-6, material rtol 1e-6/atol 1e-9 — inspect exact test helpers in the slice3 continuation module).\n- Nonzero plasticity, target error <= 1e-8.\n- Independent copied-H reduced residual <= 1e-7, original constraints/reaction wrench <= 1e-6 using established test-only oracle in original continuation module.\n- Example must NOT import tests/private assembly. Tests can use original oracle or load checkpoint data through public existing serializer.\n- Retain raw full/prefix/resume checkpoints + all stdout.\n- Rejected-increment/cancellation input purity checks and failed-terminal restart refusal.\n- Can reuse existi