ckpoint-out` → allowed (that's the completed-prefix checkpoint).\n\nValidation rules (before solve):\n- `--checkpoint-out` requires `--case plastic` → error \"--checkpoint-out is only supported for the registered plastic case\"\n- `--restart` requires `--case plastic` → \"--restart is only delivered for the registered plastic case\"\n- `--restart` requires `--stage full` → \"--restart resumes toward the full target; --stage must be full\"\n- restart path must exist and be a file → clear message.\n\nExit codes: 0 completed; 1 non-completion (cancelled/stopped/refused); 2 argparse/flag validation errors. I'll implement flag validation via parser.error() (exit 2, message to stderr) — \"Reject incompatible flag combinations clearly before solve\". parser.error is the cleanest.\n\nWriting checkpoint: only when completed and checkpoint_out given:\n```python\nraw = result.restart_checkpoint_bytes()\nwith open(checkpoint_out, \"wb\") as f: f.write(raw)\n```\n\"do not overwrite existing completed-prefix checkpoint on cancellation/failure\" — we only write on completion. But what if checkpoint_out exists and run completes? Overwrite is fine (it's the requested output).\n\nHmm wait, one subtlety: \"Save checkpoint via result.restart_checkpoint_bytes(), only on completed target; do not overwrite existing completed-prefix checkpoint on cancellation/failure.\" — exactly what we do.\n\nFor emit_restart_checkpoint: pass `emit_restart_checkpoint=True` for plastic runs always? If checkpoint_out is None, no need... but wait — for the resume run, restart_checkpoint param forces emit anyway. For full/prefix without checkpoint-out, emitting costs checkpoint creation (deep copies of states — small model, cheap). I'll emit only when checkpoint_out is not None to keep the default surface minimal. Hmm — but actually, is there value in always having the checkpoint in the result? The tests use CLI with --checkpoint-out always. Keep conditional.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtlety with `record